 "story": "Every board in this house knows his weight by now. \nNight pushes in, thinner than glass, \nand the only thing moving outside is the wind.\n\nBarrel steady, breath measured. \nWhoever's out there - they're patient, \nbut he's played this game longer than most.\n\nNo footsteps yet. \nJust time stretching out between heartbeats - \nthe waiting always lasts the longest.",
 "notes": "Painterly monochrome distills each detail to tension: the sharp edge of the rifle, the ghost of the outside world glimpsed through wavering glass, the gravity in his stare. Rather than posture or bravado, this portrait offers the slow, silent war of experience versus threat. \n\nCollectors will recognize the cinematic roots - think High Noon by lamplight - and appreciate the restraint: an image about waiting, knowing, and holding ground long after most would have lost their nerve. The West as endurance, distilled.",
