 "story": "The wood creaks - \nevery sound out here carries twice as far when you're waiting for something that never comes.\n\nLeaning against the wall, feeling the print above his shoulder. \nA face like his, staring back from yellowed paper - \nis it justice or just a name drawn by luck and someone else's memory?\n\nHe watches the empty road. \nNo mail, no word, \njust a price written where his story ought to be.",
 "notes": "Channeling the narrative unease of early noir Westerns and the visual legacy of photographers like Dorothea Lange and Arthur Rothstein, this image places the outlaw both inside and outside his own legend. The composition - doorway framing, boots planted, poster overhead - holds the viewer at the threshold of judgment.\n\nPainterly monochrome amplifies the mood: woodgrain, weathered poster, shadowed expression, each detail a clue but never a verdict. The outlaw's body language suggests waiting for more than mail - he's poised between past and future, bounty and belonging, name and number. \n\nFor collectors, this piece is about tension and the unresolved: who decides the story, who writes the price, and what remains when the mail never comes. A study in narrative pause, and an homage to the spaces between Western myth and memory.",
