 "story": "Feels like the sun's never been hotter. \nKeep moving - don't show pain, don't give - em the satisfaction.\n\nShoulders burning, bandolier digging in. \nDon't look back. \nNo point counting who's still with you - \ndoesn't change what needs to be done.\n\nWhoever's coming - let them. \nI'll hold this line, eyes up. \nThey'll know what they're facing, soon enough.",
 "notes": "The photograph channels the intensity of early Mexican Revolution portraiture and the cinematic directness of Sam Peckinpah's Westerns: shoulders angled, jaw set, every detail sharpened by heat and threat.\n\nPainterly monochrome heightens the drama - sunlight washing out the hat's brim, stubble and fabric rendered in stark relief, the bandolier a diagonal scar of intent. The subject's gaze is the anchor: unwavering, defiant, challenging anyone to test the line he's drawn. Collectors will find in this image a portrait of psychological endurance - a Western hero who carries not just weapons but the burden of holding ground when everything's at stake. It's grit distilled to its visual essence, and a testament to the timeless struggle etched across the frontier.",
